# One row per settled layer a remainder walk carries up, saying which end of the path's factor each
# bound of that layer's remainder took, and whether taking ends changed the figure at all.
#
# One rule at both places, and only the sign varies
#   composition/converted.sqlc and composition/settled_remainders.sqlc both take the least and the
#   greatest over the factor's two ends, so a conversion is one rule wherever it appears, and the
#   figure's sign is the only thing deciding which end a bound uses. Where the figure is not below
#   zero that comes to the plain bound-by-bound product; where it is below zero it reverses,
#   because more of a shortfall converted at a larger rate is the smaller number. This relation
#   says which of the two each filing reaches.
#
# The sign is all a conversion needs
#   A conversion multiplies by a factor that is positive, and which end of the factor a bound
#   takes depends only on the sign of the figure. A positive factor cannot change a sign, so the
#   end is the same at every level of a path, and a path of conversions is one conversion by the
#   product of its factors.
#
# What `corner_rule_bites` is for
#   It is true only where taking ends gives a different figure from multiplying bound by bound,
#   which needs a factor that is a range and a figure below zero at that bound. Either alone is not
#   enough. So the column needs both, and counting it is how a reader learns whether the least and
#   the greatest in settled_remainders are doing any work on the corpus or are standing ready for a
#   document nobody has filed.
#
# Reading it all false
#   All false does not mean the rule about ends is unnecessary. It means the two populations that
#   would exercise it, layers under a factor that is a range and layers whose remainder is below
#   zero, do not overlap on the corpus. Both are sizeable and what they share is empty, which is a
#   fact about the evidence and not about the arithmetic.
#
# It reports and never accuses
#   A remainder below zero is ordinary: it is a layer whose demand exceeds its supply, the
#   interference case the model exists to measure. A factor that is a range is ordinary. There is
#   nothing here for a rule to find, which is why this is a relation and there is no check beside
#   it.
#
# The arithmetic behind the verdict is carried, not just the verdict
#   `corner_rule_bites` is a comparison, so the two figures it compares come along beside it: the
#   converted bounds this walk gave and what multiplying bound by bound would have given instead.
#   No reader in these queries needs them, and they stay because this relation runs on its own and
#   a verdict whose inputs are not in the row can only be trusted. The factor and the figure's most
#   likely value are here for the same reason: a conversion is read at three values, and showing
#   two of them invites the question of what the third did.
#
# The figure is read before conversion
#   The sign has to come from before the multiplication, so the layer's own difference is joined
#   rather than recovered by dividing the converted figure back out. A positive factor keeps the
#   sign, so the two agree, and joining is the reading that does not depend on that.
-- composition/settled_remainders.sqlc against layers/differenced_remainder.sqlc at the node the
-- walk stops on, one row per settled node.
SELECT s.composition, s.composed_layer, s.node_filing, s.node_layer, s.depth,
       s.factor_low, s.factor_mode, s.factor_high, s.spread_factor,
       b.r_low  AS operand_low,
       b.r_mode AS operand_mode,
       b.r_high AS operand_high,
       s.r_low  AS converted_low,
       s.r_mode AS converted_mode,
       s.r_high AS converted_high,
       b.r_low  * s.factor_low  AS bound_by_bound_low,
       b.r_high * s.factor_high AS bound_by_bound_high,
       CASE WHEN b.r_low  < 0 THEN 'the factor high' ELSE 'the factor low'  END AS low_took,
       CASE WHEN b.r_high < 0 THEN 'the factor low'  ELSE 'the factor high' END AS high_took,
       (abs(s.r_low  - b.r_low  * s.factor_low)  > 1e-9
        OR abs(s.r_high - b.r_high * s.factor_high) > 1e-9) AS corner_rule_bites
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/settled_remainders.sqlc)
) s
JOIN      (
    :compose(layers/differenced_remainder.sqlc)
) b ON b.filing = s.node_filing AND b.layer = s.node_layer
